    {
      "evidence_id": "pdfplumber:abstract",
      "source": "pdf_layout",
      "field": "abstract",
      "raw_text": "HTTP Request Smuggling (HRS) is an attack that exploits the HTTP processing discrepancies between two servers deployed in a proxy- origin configuration, allowing attackers to smuggle hidden requests through the proxy. While this idea is not new, HRS is soaring in popularity due to recently revealed novel exploitation techniques and real-life abuse scenarios. In this work, we step back from the highly-specific exploits hog- ging the spotlight, and present the first work that systematically explores HRS within a scientific framework. We design an experi- ment infrastructure powered by a novel grammar-based differential fuzzer, test 10 popular server/proxy/CDN technologies in combi- nations, identify pairs that result in processing discrepancies, and discover exploits that lead to HRS. Our experiment reveals previ- ously unknown ways to manipulate HTTP requests for exploitation, and for the first time documents the server pairs prone to HRS.",
      "extractor": "pdfplumber_section_boundary"
    },
